If you’re deploying CVAT on AWS EKS, the best and most efficient storage option is Amazon EFS, not S3.

Below is a clear decision, followed by step-by-step implementation for the recommended approach (EFS) and when S3 makes sense.

✅ Final Recommendation (Short Answer)
Use case	Best Option
CVAT persistent data (annotations, uploads, logs, DB, Redis)	✅ Amazon EFS
Shared storage across multiple pods/nodes	✅ Amazon EFS
Low latency, POSIX filesystem required	✅ Amazon EFS
Cheap long-term archive / backups / exports	Amazon S3 (secondary)

Production-grade CVAT on EKS = EFS (Primary) + S3 (Optional Backup)

❌ Why S3 Alone Is NOT Good for CVAT

CVAT requires:

POSIX file system

File locking

Fast random read/write

Shared access between pods

S3 is object storage, not a filesystem.

❌ Problems with S3:

No POSIX support

High latency
CVAT officially recommends filesystem storage

👉 Never use S3 directly as CVAT primary volume

✅ Why Amazon EFS Is Best for CVAT

✔ POSIX-compliant
✔ RWX (ReadWriteMany)
✔ Shared across nodes
✔ Auto-scaling storage
✔ No manual disk management
✔ Works perfectly with Kubernetes

Needs hacks like s3fs / goofys (not recommended for production)
